# Yolo media node (service/node). The root .env is loaded automatically;
# exported variables take precedence. YOLO_ENV_FILE points at another file
# (an empty value loads none). The control plane's settings live elsewhere:
# yolo.instance.json for an instance, service/control/.dev.vars for development.

# Shared with the control plane's NODE_SECRET Worker secret: signs control
# plane requests and the media tokens in /m/{token}/… URLs. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# NODE_SECRET=
# NODE_HOST=127.0.0.1
# NODE_PORT=9191
# Unix socket where `npm run owner-code` asks the node for a pairing code; never
# reachable through the tunnel. Default: .yolo-node.sock in the repository, or
# /tmp/yolo-<uid>/<hash>.sock when that path is too long for a socket.
# NODE_LOCAL_SOCKET=/absolute/path/to/yolo-node.sock
# Control plane address that `npm run owner-code` prints as the sign-in URL:
# the custom domain, or the workers.dev host without one (`npm run yolo --
# init` sets it)
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://yolo.example.com
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Media pipeline (the node serves /m/*). Caches go to .segment-cache/,
# .hls-cache/ and .media-cache/ under YOLO_RUNTIME_DIR (default: the repository).
# YOLO_RUNTIME_DIR=/absolute/path/for/caches
# The yt-dlp and Deno builds tools.json pins: `npm install` downloads them into
# a cache shared by every checkout (default ~/Library/Caches/yolo/tools on
# macOS, $XDG_CACHE_HOME/yolo/tools or ~/.cache/yolo/tools on Linux) and links
# them in .tools/bin, which the node puts first on its PATH. These two are read
# from the environment of `npm install`, not from this file; YOLO_TOOLS=skip
# skips the step.
# YOLO_TOOLS_CACHE=/absolute/path/for/tools
# YOLO_TOOLS=skip
# SEGMENT_CACHE_MAX_BYTES=21474836480
# Video bitrate ceiling on the relay through Cloudflare, and for the node's
# own transcodes on it (500-20000). LAN_MAX_VIDEO_KBPS and WAN_MAX_VIDEO_KBPS
# set the direct paths' ceilings; a screen's adaptive quality lowers any of
# them after a stall
# STREAM_MAX_VIDEO_BITRATE_KBPS=4800
# Transcode separate audio/video files with ffmpeg instead of serving them as
# fMP4 HLS over byte ranges
# DASH_TRANSCODE=0

# LAN direct play (Agent Note 2026-10-12-lan-direct-play). Once the control
# plane has a DNS provider (the MEDIA_DNS_TOKEN secret, or a DNS provider
# plugin's name and secret below), it names this node, points a DNS-only
# record at its private address, and installs a certificate; the node then
# serves /m/* to devices at home over HTTPS on that address. The key and the
# certificate live in .lan/ under YOLO_RUNTIME_DIR.
# Port of the LAN listener; 0 turns LAN direct play off on this node
# LAN_PORT=9443
# Pin the private IPv4 address to listen on (default: the first private
# address of a wired or Wi-Fi interface)
# LAN_ADDRESS=192.168.1.20
# Video bitrate ceiling on the LAN path (default 200000, effectively none)
# LAN_MAX_VIDEO_KBPS=200000
# Bonjour (Agent Note 2026-10-19-client-protocol): the node advertises
# _yolo._tcp on the home network with the control plane's origin (PUBLIC_ORIGIN,
# else the one the LAN or remote listener allows), through dns-sd on macOS or
# avahi-publish on Linux, so a TV app at home finds the address to pair with;
# 0 turns it off
# BONJOUR=1

# Remote direct play (Agent Note 2026-10-13-media-off-the-worker). Once the
# owner turns on remote access in settings, the control plane names this node
# a second time, points a DNS-only record at the home's public address, and
# installs a certificate; the node then serves /m/* to devices anywhere over
# HTTPS through a router port. Without a domain (WAN_CERTIFICATE "ip" on the
# Worker) the node instead obtains a certificate for the home's public
# address itself and serves https://<address>:<port>. The key, certificate,
# and that ACME account live in .lan/wan/.
# Port of the remote listener; 0 turns remote direct play off on this node
# WAN_PORT=9444
# auto: the node asks the router for the port mapping (PCP, then NAT-PMP,
# then UPnP IGD), renews it, and removes it when it stops. manual: the owner
# forwards the port on the router and the node sends the router nothing.
# WAN_MAPPING=auto
# The router's external port: the one asked for (auto; the router may give
# another), or the one the owner forwarded (manual). Default: WAN_PORT
# WAN_EXTERNAL_PORT=9444
# The router's address (default: the default gateway)
# WAN_GATEWAY=192.168.1.1
# Pin the private IPv4 address the router forwards to (default: LAN_ADDRESS's)
# WAN_ADDRESS=192.168.1.20
# Also listen on the machine's global IPv6 address, which the control plane
# publishes as AAAA: "on" (the first global address of a wired or Wi-Fi
# interface) or the address itself, once the router's IPv6 firewall lets TCP
# WAN_PORT through to it. Default off. Without a domain the certificate
# covers this address too while the CA reaches the node on its port 80
# WAN_IPV6=on
# Without a domain: the node's port for the CA's HTTP-01 check, which the
# router's port 80 reaches; it listens only while an order runs. 0 leaves
# only TLS-ALPN-01, which needs WAN_EXTERNAL_PORT=443
# WAN_CHALLENGE_PORT=9480
# auto: the node asks the router for port 80 only while an order runs.
# manual: the owner forwards TCP port 80 to WAN_CHALLENGE_PORT. Default:
# WAN_MAPPING's value
# WAN_CHALLENGE_MAPPING=auto
# Video bitrate ceiling on remote direct play, which the home's upload
# carries (default 20000; at least 500)
# WAN_MAX_VIDEO_KBPS=20000
#
# The control plane's side is configured on the Worker, not here:
#   MEDIA_DNS_TOKEN     secret, only with a domain: a Cloudflare API token
#                       with Zone Read and DNS Edit on the zone that holds
#                       PUBLIC_ORIGIN's host. `npm run yolo -- provision
#                       media-dns` creates and stores it; by hand, npm run
#                       wrangler -- secret put MEDIA_DNS_TOKEN. LAN_DNS_TOKEN,
#                       its name before 0.5, is no longer read
#   LAN_ACME_DIRECTORY  var in wrangler.jsonc: the ACME directory (Let's
#                       Encrypt production there; staging when unset); without
#                       a domain the node uses the one the control plane sends
#   WAN_CERTIFICATE     var rendered from the instance's media.wan.certificate:
#                       "name" (a name in the zone) or "ip" (no domain and no
#                       DNS provider plugin)
#   MEDIA_DNS_PROVIDER, MEDIA_DNS_ZONE
#                       vars rendered from the instance's media.dns: the
#                       DNS provider of the node's media names, "cloudflare"
#                       (the domain's zone, MEDIA_DNS_TOKEN) by default, or a
#                       plugin's, such as "desec", with the name they go
#                       under, such as home.dedyn.io
#   DESEC_TOKEN         secret, only with media.dns.provider "desec" (the
#                       dns-desec plugin): a deSEC API token for that name.
#                       The owner runs `npm run yolo -- provision media-dns
#                       --token-stdin` in their own terminal, which checks it
#                       with deSEC and stores it without showing it

# Plex source plugin (optional; its options in yolo.config.json win)
# PLEX_URL=http://localhost:32400
# PLEX_TOKEN=your-plex-token
# Plex transcodes for the car use a fixed 720p profile, which a browser also
# gets under a ceiling below 8 Mbps, as on the relay; on the LAN and remote
# direct play a browser gets its screen's picture size (and an original on the
# LAN path plays as it is). The optional override of the 720p bitrate is
# limited to 4000-4800 Kbps.
# PLEX_VIDEO_BITRATE_KBPS=4800

# Folder source plugin (Agent Note 2026-10-26-folder-source; its options in
# yolo.config.json win): folders on this machine whose movies and shows Yolo
# lists and plays without Plex, such as a disk or a NAS share mounted here.
# Absolute paths separated by ":" (";" on Windows). Their movies form one
# library and their shows another. The node plays a file only from inside these folders. Without any, the
# plugin lists nothing. ffmpeg plays what a screen cannot take as it is
# (FFMPEG_PATH, default ffmpeg on PATH); ffprobe reads the files (FFPROBE_PATH
# below)
# FOLDER_ROOTS=/Volumes/Media/Movies:/Volumes/Media/TV
# FFMPEG_PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin/ffmpeg

# yt-dlp source plugin authentication (optional; default is anonymous, no
# browser required). Set one when the source needs login or browser cookies,
# such as Bilibili's subtitles, which it gives only to a signed-in account.
# A file takes precedence.
# YTDLP_COOKIES_FROM_BROWSER=chrome
# YTDLP_COOKIES_FILE=/absolute/path/to/cookies.txt

# Claude Code agent plugin (optional; see Agent Note
# 2026-10-18-agent-from-the-screen): the owner's screens ask the Claude Code
# signed in on this machine, through its MCP server named "yolo" (add it with
# user scope, as skills/yolo-connect-agent says). No API key is needed.
# CLAUDE_CODE_COMMAND=/absolute/path/to/claude
# CLAUDE_CODE_YOLO_SERVER=yolo
# CLAUDE_CODE_MODEL=sonnet

# Codex agent plugin (optional; see Agent Note 2026-10-18-agent-from-the-screen):
# the same for the Codex CLI signed in on this machine, through its MCP server
# named "yolo" (`codex mcp add yolo --url …`, then `codex mcp login yolo`).
# Each screen chooses its agent in Settings; without a choice, the first one
# loaded that can answer does.
# CODEX_COMMAND=/absolute/path/to/codex
# CODEX_YOLO_SERVER=yolo
# CODEX_MODEL=gpt-5

# AI subtitles plugin (optional, not loaded by default; see Agent Note
# 2026-10-18-ai-subtitles): add plugins/subtitles-openai to yolo.config.json
# with the `url` of a speech server that speaks OpenAI's interfaces, such as
# one on another Mac at home. A key only if that server wants one.
# SUBTITLES_OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Egress proxies (optional; see Agent Note 2026-10-13-node-egress-proxy): the
# "egress" section of yolo.config.json sends the hosts its rules list through
# named proxies, every other host direct. The proxy-url plugin
# (plugins/proxy-url, not loaded by default) reads a fixed proxy, its
# credentials included, from the variable a proxy's options.env names, such as
#   "proxies": { "abroad": { "provider": "url", "options": { "env": "EGRESS_ABROAD_PROXY_URL" } } }
# EGRESS_ABROAD_PROXY_URL=http://user:password@proxy.example:8080

# Language models for plugins (see Agent Note 2026-10-25-models-for-plugins).
# The model-subscriptions plugin borrows the sign-in of the Codex or Claude
# Code on this machine, as Ask runs them, so it needs nothing here; without
# either, `npm run yolo -- models login` signs Yolo in itself. Each plugin
# makes at most this many model calls a day.
# MODEL_CALLS_PER_DAY=500
# The key of a custom model server (the plugin's custom.url), if it wants one
# MODEL_CUSTOM_API_KEY=

# Metadata (see Agent Note 2026-10-25-metadata-plugins): posters, synopses,
# and cast for every item, and which episode a web upload is. The language of
# titles and synopses, as a BCP 47 tag
# YOLO_METADATA_LANGUAGE=en
# TV details come without a key, from Wikidata and Wikipedia, TVmaze, and
# Bangumi. Optional: a key of the owner's own from The Movie Database adds
# films the others miss, backdrops, and better posters; register a free one at
# https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api (a v3 API key or a v4 read access
# token). Without one the TMDB plugin does nothing
# TMDB_API_KEY=
# ffprobe, for tags embedded in files (the local metadata plugin) and for
# reading the folder source's files (its streams, and keyframes for the node)
# FFPROBE_PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin/ffprobe

# Yolo plugins (see Agent Note 2026-10-11-plugin-architecture).
# Without a config, the node loads built-in link playback (source-ytdlp,
# which reads YTDLP_* above) and nothing else. The owner turns on more from
# any screen (Settings > Plugins, or the home's "Where do you usually
# watch?"), or with npm run yolo -- plugins on <name>; the node then writes
# yolo.config.json (ignored by git) and applies it without a restart. The
# plugins read PLEX_*, CLAUDE_CODE_*, CODEX_*, MODEL_*, and TMDB_API_KEY above
# unless their options say otherwise. Editing the file by hand (copy
# yolo.config.example.json) needs a node restart, and a redeploy of the
# control plane for a plugin with a control entry. YOLO_CONFIG points
# elsewhere, relative to the repository. The control plane's build reads the
# same file.
# YOLO_CONFIG=/absolute/path/to/yolo.config.json

# Player development (npm run dev): where Vite sends Yolo's routes
# YOLO_CONTROL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787
